lesson: 184
title: Two-digit numbers with a given digit sum
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A two-digit number has a tens digit from 1 to 9 and a ones digit from 0 to 9; count the numbers by trying each tens digit in turn.
explain: Digit sum 5: tens 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 give 14, 23, 32, 41, 50, that is 5 numbers. For a sum up to 9 there are as many numbers as the sum.
explain: Digit sum 13: the ones digit can be at most 9, so the tens digit is at least 4: 49, 58, 67, 76, 85, 94, that is 6 numbers, 19 - 13.
vars: a=name s=2..18
answer: (s <= 9) * s + (s > 9) * (19 - s)
exercise: How many two-digit numbers have digits that add up to {s}?
exercise: {a} writes every two-digit number whose digit sum is {s}. How many numbers does {a.he} write?
exercise: The digits of a two-digit number add up to {s}. How many such numbers are there?
exercise: How many numbers from 10 to 99 have a digit sum of {s}?
exercise: {a} thinks of a two-digit number. Its two digits add up to {s}. How many different numbers could it be?
test: Among the whole numbers between 10 and 99, for how many is the sum of the tens digit and the ones digit equal to {s}?
test: A two-digit house number has the property that its digits sum to {s}. How many house numbers have this property?
test: {a} counts all the two-digit numbers in which the first digit plus the second digit makes {s}. What number does {a.he} get?
exercise: How many numbers between 10 and 99 have digits with a sum of {s}?
exercise: {a} in Ohio lists all the two-digit numbers whose two digits add to {s}. How many numbers are on the list?
exercise: Two-digit numbers with digit sum {s}: how many are there?
exercise: A two-digit locker code in a gym in Sydney has digits that add up to {s}, and its first digit is not 0. How many codes are possible?
exercise: The sum of the two digits of a two-digit number is {s}. How many different two-digit numbers can that be?
exercise: {a} picks a two-digit number with a digit sum of {s.words}. How many choices does {a.he} have?
exercise: In a game in Toronto, every card shows a different two-digit number whose digits total {s}. How many cards can there be?
exercise: Count the two-digit numbers in which the tens digit and the ones digit together make {s}.
test: A bus route number in Dublin has two digits, and the digits add to {s}. How many such route numbers are possible?
test: {a} adds up the two digits of every number from 10 to 99. How many times does {a.he} get {s}?
test: Room numbers in a hotel in Melbourne go from 10 to 99. How many of the rooms have numbers whose digits sum to {s}?
test: A tens digit and a ones digit are chosen for a two-digit number so that they total {s}. In how many ways can it be done?
test: A secret two-digit number in New Zealand has digits summing to {s}. How many numbers must {a} try, at most, to be sure to hit it?
test: For how many two-digit numbers is the digit sum exactly {s}?
exercise: how many 2-digit numbers got digits adding to {s}
exercise: {a} lists every 2 digit number with digit sum {s}. how many is that
exercise: two-digit numbers, digits add to {s+1} — no wait, {s}. how many are there
exercise: digits add up to {s}, number's 2 digits (teacher gave us 10 mins lol). how many such numbers
test: Sam says {s+1} of em. nah. how many two-digit numbers have digit sum {s}
test: count all the numbers 10 to 99 whose digits sum to {s}
test: {a}'s locker code is 2 digits, first one not 0, digits add to {s}. how many codes could it be
exercise: Grandad passed away, and the code of his safe is a two-digit number whose digits add up to {s}. How many codes might the family have to try?
exercise: uncle croaked and his lockbox code's 2 digits adding to {s}. how many codes we gotta try, for crying out loud
test: The bank was economical with the truth about its "unbreakable" lock: the code is a two-digit number with digit sum {s}. How many such codes are possible?
exam: Determine the cardinality of the set of two-digit numbers the sum of whose digits is equal to {s}.
exam: It is given that {a} records every two-digit number whose digits sum to {s}. Ascertain the number of numbers so recorded.
exam: State how many whole numbers between 10 and 99 inclusive possess the property that the sum of their two digits amounts to {s.words}.
exam: Whereas a locker number consists of exactly two digits, the first of which is not zero, and the sum of its digits is {s}, determine the number of distinct locker numbers satisfying these conditions.
